  "notes": "Funk has no section of its own in the compendium, so the numbers are extrapolated from the ghost-note and live-kit bullets of ch.1 §9, the Dorian-vamp harmony of ch.2 §6 and the Neptunes entry that carries the modern lineage — hence medium confidence. What makes it itself: the ghost hand. A hard 2 and 4 with nearly every remaining e and a brushed underneath at a fifth of the backbeat's velocity, which is the densest ghost-snare part in this dataset by a wide margin. The kick states the one emphatically and then moves off the grid onto the 16ths; the hats run a continuous 16th stream with the 8ths accented hard over the quiet ones. There is no 808 — the bass is a played instrument with a figure of its own, the busiest line here, ranging three octaves and answering the horns rather than doubling the kick. Harmony is a one- or two-chord Dorian or Mixolydian vamp with a seventh or a ninth on nearly everything, and the melody is a horn-section stab: staccato, doubled in thirds, rhythmic rather than lyrical.",
